# Architecture boundaries

Backend (`apps/api/src/`) follows Ports & Adapters (Hexagonal Architecture).
Dependency flow: `Presentation → Application → Ports → Infrastructure`,
wired by `composition/`.

| Layer          | Location                        | May import                     | Must NEVER import           |
| -------------- | -------------------------------- | ------------------------------- | ---------------------------- |
| Presentation   | `presentation/`                  | Use cases, schemas, contracts   | Prisma, concrete repos       |
| Application    | `application/<feature>/`         | Ports, read models, contracts   | Prisma, Hono, concrete repos |
| Infrastructure | `infrastructure/`                 | Prisma, ports, read models      | Hono, routes                 |
| Composition    | `composition/`                    | Everything (wiring edge only)   | —                             |

- `@prisma/client` only inside `infrastructure/`.
- Concrete repository classes only imported in `composition/container.ts`.
- `packages/contracts` is the single source of truth for DTOs — never duplicate
  or redefine API types elsewhere.
- No business logic in route handlers or `page.tsx`.
- No singleton repository defaults — wire dependencies in `composition/container.ts`.

## Naming

Use descriptive, unabbreviated names: `CreateApplicationUseCase`,
`ListApplicationsUseCase`, `JobApplicationsRepository`. Avoid abbreviations.

## Feature implementation order

When a feature spans layers, implement in this order and never skip a layer:

1. `packages/contracts` (DTOs)
2. Application (ports + use cases)
3. Infrastructure (Prisma repo + mapper)
4. Composition (register in `container.ts`)
5. Presentation (route + Zod schema)
6. Frontend (`src/lib/api/*` client → feature UI)
7. Tests

See `docs/architecture.md` for the full layer diagram.
